WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.316 --> 00:00:02.860
- He's an American businessman, investor,

00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:05.628
television personality,
and philanthropist.

00:00:05.628 --> 00:00:08.072
- He's the owner of the
NBA Dallas Mavericks

00:00:08.072 --> 00:00:09.548
and Landmark Theaters.

00:00:09.548 --> 00:00:13.031
- He has an estimated net
worth of $3 billion US.

00:00:13.031 --> 00:00:15.214
- He is Mark Cuban, and here are

00:00:15.214 --> 00:00:17.385
his top ten rules for success.

00:00:17.385 --> 00:00:18.529
(whoosh)

00:00:18.529 --> 00:00:20.446
(thump)

00:00:20.446 --> 00:00:21.846
- You know, personally,
I think every time's

00:00:21.846 --> 00:00:23.181
a perfect time to start a business.

00:00:23.181 --> 00:00:24.368
There's no bad times.

00:00:24.368 --> 00:00:27.391
If you do the work, if
you do the preparation,

00:00:27.391 --> 00:00:28.781
you'll know when it's time.

00:00:28.781 --> 00:00:31.532
And it doesn't mean that it
won't be a little bit scary,

00:00:31.532 --> 00:00:33.782
but, you know, you'll know.

00:00:35.426 --> 00:00:37.920
And you don't have to quit the daytime job

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:39.894
if you don't feel all that comfortable,

00:00:39.894 --> 00:00:41.137
and you can give it a run at night.

00:00:41.137 --> 00:00:42.789
But whatever works for you.

00:00:42.789 --> 00:00:45.299
Now, with the Internet, you've got

00:00:45.299 --> 00:00:46.673
all the choices in the world,

00:00:46.673 --> 00:00:50.107
and you can just go out there
and do your own thing and,

00:00:50.107 --> 00:00:51.921
you know, set up a business part-time.

00:00:51.921 --> 00:00:54.505
The passion I've always had for business

00:00:54.505 --> 00:00:58.372
and being an entrepreneur,
that transfers into the Mavs.

00:00:58.372 --> 00:01:00.310
I've always been passionate.

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:01.506
Some people thought, you know,

00:01:01.506 --> 00:01:04.066
it's more OCD than anything else,

00:01:04.066 --> 00:01:08.041
which I think is a great
trait for an entrepreneur.

00:01:08.041 --> 00:01:10.075
When I, you know, I
mentioned the stamp business,

00:01:10.075 --> 00:01:12.577
I would stay up 'till
three, four in the morning

00:01:12.577 --> 00:01:14.341
even though I had to
get up and go to school

00:01:14.341 --> 00:01:17.990
and read Linn's Stamp News
and Scott's Stamp Journals

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:20.579
and to have them all
memorized and use that

00:01:20.579 --> 00:01:22.472
to give myself an edge.

00:01:22.472 --> 00:01:26.401
Even when I was in college,
I'd be in the library

00:01:26.401 --> 00:01:27.994
reading business books and just looking

00:01:27.994 --> 00:01:30.261
for business biographies and just reading

00:01:30.261 --> 00:01:32.327
all I could about business.

00:01:32.327 --> 00:01:35.738
When I had MicroSolutions and, you know,

00:01:35.738 --> 00:01:37.841
I started with no money, you know.

00:01:37.841 --> 00:01:41.627
I'd pull all-nighters in
front of borrowed computers

00:01:41.627 --> 00:01:45.038
teaching myself software
and how to program.

00:01:45.038 --> 00:01:46.720
Just to go after it.

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:48.494
I mean, the thing about
being an entrepreneur

00:01:48.494 --> 00:01:50.948
is it's just all to you.

00:01:50.948 --> 00:01:52.716
You know, a lot of people
like to make excuses,

00:01:52.716 --> 00:01:54.176
I don't have connections,
I don't have money,

00:01:54.176 --> 00:01:55.009
I don't have this.

00:01:55.009 --> 00:01:57.245
But, you know, if you find something

00:01:57.245 --> 00:01:59.643
that you like to do or love to do,

00:01:59.643 --> 00:02:02.394
be great at it and see if you
can turn it into a business.

00:02:02.394 --> 00:02:04.444
And worst case, you're
going to have fun doing

00:02:04.444 --> 00:02:05.778
what it is you love to do, and best case,

00:02:05.778 --> 00:02:07.084
you can turn it into a business.

00:02:07.084 --> 00:02:08.824
I'm just not big on excuses.

00:02:08.824 --> 00:02:10.001
I just think if you're really,

00:02:10.001 --> 00:02:11.908
everybody has that
opportunity to go for it.

00:02:11.908 --> 00:02:12.869
They've just got to do it.

00:02:12.869 --> 00:02:16.193
One of the things that
companies do or startups do,

00:02:16.193 --> 00:02:19.423
they come up with an
idea, they'll Google it,

00:02:19.423 --> 00:02:22.001
and if they don't see it
in the first two pages,

00:02:22.001 --> 00:02:23.618
they think it's original.

00:02:23.618 --> 00:02:27.636
You got to go back, right,
because over the past 15 years,

00:02:27.636 --> 00:02:29.173
there's so many different businesses

00:02:29.173 --> 00:02:30.569
that have have tried and failed.

00:02:30.569 --> 00:02:33.286
You have to go back and find
those and learn from those.

00:02:33.286 --> 00:02:35.619
So you've got to understand
all the implications,

00:02:35.619 --> 00:02:37.088
and you have to learn from history.

00:02:37.088 --> 00:02:39.116
And so the best advice I can give you

00:02:39.116 --> 00:02:42.103
on a video before talking
to you or emailing with you

00:02:42.103 --> 00:02:45.822
is that you've got to find out the history

00:02:45.822 --> 00:02:47.702
of people who have tried your idea.

00:02:47.702 --> 00:02:51.227
Because there's a 99.99999% chance

00:02:51.227 --> 00:02:53.187
that your idea has been tried before.

00:02:53.187 --> 00:02:55.038
That's not a good reason not to start it,

00:02:55.038 --> 00:02:56.715
'cause you might be
able to outperform them,

00:02:56.715 --> 00:03:00.224
but you better learn from
the history of your idea.

00:03:00.224 --> 00:03:01.944
Because you know what
they say about people

00:03:01.944 --> 00:03:04.111
who don't learn from history.

00:03:04.111 --> 00:03:06.244
I've always just really enjoyed

00:03:06.244 --> 00:03:08.960
just the competition of business.

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:11.069
I think, you know, in the sports business,

00:03:11.069 --> 00:03:14.974
I'll talk to our players,
and it'll be like,

00:03:14.974 --> 00:03:16.576
well, you guys compete for 48 minutes

00:03:16.576 --> 00:03:17.850
and you practice a couple hours

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:20.709
and you work on your game
independently a couple hours.

00:03:20.709 --> 00:03:23.279
But the ultimate sport is business,

00:03:23.279 --> 00:03:26.032
because you have to
compete with everybody.

00:03:26.032 --> 00:03:30.199
And you have to do a 24 by seven
by 365 days a year forever.

00:03:31.449 --> 00:03:33.239
And there's always somebody out there

00:03:33.239 --> 00:03:34.491
trying to kick your butt.

00:03:34.491 --> 00:03:36.998
There's always somebody who
looks at your business and says,

00:03:36.998 --> 00:03:39.218
I can do that better,
I have a better idea.

00:03:39.218 --> 00:03:41.767
And you have to compete with that person,

00:03:41.767 --> 00:03:43.444
and all the while, you have to make

00:03:43.444 --> 00:03:46.158
your customers happy,
your employees happy.

00:03:46.158 --> 00:03:50.895
It's the competitive side of
me that, in any entrepreneur,

00:03:50.895 --> 00:03:53.615
that I think that that has to drive you

00:03:53.615 --> 00:03:56.204
and I think that carries
over into the Mavericks.

00:03:56.204 --> 00:03:58.657
I want to win, and I want to compete.

00:03:58.657 --> 00:04:00.735
99% of small businesses you can start

00:04:00.735 --> 00:04:02.128
with next to no capital.

00:04:02.128 --> 00:04:03.253
It's more about effort.

00:04:03.253 --> 00:04:05.965
You know, small businesses
don't fail for lack of capital.

00:04:05.965 --> 00:04:08.629
They fail for lack of brains,
they fail for lack of effort.

00:04:08.629 --> 00:04:09.721
Most people just aren't willing

00:04:09.721 --> 00:04:12.429
to put in the time to work smart.

00:04:12.429 --> 00:04:15.564
I mean, they go for it in a lot of cases,

00:04:15.564 --> 00:04:18.335
but they just don't recognize
how much work's involved

00:04:18.335 --> 00:04:20.988
and if you do the
preparation, if you know,

00:04:20.988 --> 00:04:24.041
if you start a business, you'd
better know your industry

00:04:24.041 --> 00:04:26.541
and your company better than
anyone in the whole wide world.

00:04:26.541 --> 00:04:28.709
Because you're competing, and to think

00:04:28.709 --> 00:04:30.538
that whoever it is you're competing with

00:04:30.538 --> 00:04:32.946
is just going to let you come
in and take their business,

00:04:32.946 --> 00:04:34.304
obviously that's naive.

00:04:34.304 --> 00:04:36.336
And I think most people
don't recognize that.

00:04:36.336 --> 00:04:38.460
If you're going to compete with
me and one of my businesses,

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:39.932
you better realize that I'm working

00:04:39.932 --> 00:04:42.207
24 hours a day to kick your ass.

00:04:42.207 --> 00:04:45.110
One of the big things that all startups do

00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:48.839
is they lie to themselves
over and over and over.

00:04:48.839 --> 00:04:51.722
Mine's faster, mine's
cheaper, mine's better.

00:04:51.722 --> 00:04:53.300
Mine's this, mine's that.

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:54.944
No it's not.

00:04:54.944 --> 00:04:56.876
And the reason it's not is because

00:04:56.876 --> 00:04:58.682
whoever it is you're competing with,

00:04:58.682 --> 00:05:00.265
it's not like they're ignoring you.

00:05:00.265 --> 00:05:02.676
It's not like, oh my goodness,

00:05:02.676 --> 00:05:05.816
this guy just started on Shopify
in the startup competition,

00:05:05.816 --> 00:05:07.465
he's doing $1 million this year.

00:05:07.465 --> 00:05:10.250
Woe is me, I might as
well close up the doors.

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:11.157
What are they doing?

00:05:11.157 --> 00:05:12.273
I'm going to copy what they're doing.

00:05:12.273 --> 00:05:13.740
And now you've got to stay ahead.

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:16.011
And so, you know, you've
got to be very careful

00:05:16.011 --> 00:05:19.385
as an entrepreneur to be
brutally honest with yourself.

00:05:19.385 --> 00:05:20.801
And those are some of the things

00:05:20.801 --> 00:05:22.668
that you'll hear from me as a mentor,

00:05:22.668 --> 00:05:24.577
that, you know, know what you know,

00:05:24.577 --> 00:05:25.588
know what you don't know,

00:05:25.588 --> 00:05:28.182
but you've got to know your
business better than anybody.

00:05:28.182 --> 00:05:31.793
I think the most important
is knowing your strengths

00:05:31.793 --> 00:05:35.793
and weaknesses and knowing
what you enjoy doing.

00:05:37.045 --> 00:05:40.174
You know, if you look at it
as a job, you've already lost.

00:05:40.174 --> 00:05:42.027
It's not going to be your passion,

00:05:42.027 --> 00:05:43.780
and you're going to count the hours.

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:47.530
If you look at it as
something you love to do

00:05:49.328 --> 00:05:51.307
and then you know what your strengths are,

00:05:51.307 --> 00:05:53.866
then you can leverage those strengths

00:05:53.866 --> 00:05:55.840
in your business and in helping others,

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:58.335
and once you recognize your weaknesses,

00:05:58.335 --> 00:06:01.030
then you can work with
people that complement you.

00:06:01.030 --> 00:06:02.802
I mean, every one of my businesses,

00:06:02.802 --> 00:06:05.159
I've had a partner who's very anal.

00:06:05.159 --> 00:06:06.840
(laughs)

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:09.112
Martin Woodall, Todd Wagner.

00:06:09.112 --> 00:06:12.059
I mean, incredibly anal
people, perfectionists.

00:06:12.059 --> 00:06:13.433
'Cause I'm a slob, you know.

00:06:13.433 --> 00:06:16.455
I'm a big picture, think about
what's around the corner,

00:06:16.455 --> 00:06:18.272
how's technology going to change things,

00:06:18.272 --> 00:06:20.805
how can I change, you know, this industry.

00:06:20.805 --> 00:06:23.110
And, you know, making sure
that there's somebody there

00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:28.100
to dot the Is, cross the Ts,
and keep me in the baselines.

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:30.867
And, you know, recognizing my weaknesses

00:06:30.867 --> 00:06:33.817
is just as important as
recognizing my strengths,

00:06:33.817 --> 00:06:36.610
my core competencies, and, you know,

00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:38.270
having a passion to do them.

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:39.877
You've got to be
differentiated and unique.

00:06:39.877 --> 00:06:41.691
You've got to know what
your core competency is

00:06:41.691 --> 00:06:42.651
and be great at it.

00:06:42.651 --> 00:06:45.235
I think what people fail to realize

00:06:45.235 --> 00:06:49.402
is there's got to be a defining
feature of your company

00:06:50.355 --> 00:06:52.531
and you've got to be the
best in the world at that.

00:06:52.531 --> 00:06:54.860
Whatever industry you're
in, if you don't know

00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:57.341
more about it than
anybody else in the world,

00:06:57.341 --> 00:06:59.074
whoever those other people are that more

00:06:59.074 --> 00:07:00.064
are going to kick your ass.

00:07:00.064 --> 00:07:03.646
I had a job out of
college working for a bank

00:07:03.646 --> 00:07:05.761
for a short period of time, Mellon Bank.

00:07:05.761 --> 00:07:08.261
And I decided to, well, first,

00:07:10.476 --> 00:07:11.976
it might have been Inc. Magazine.

00:07:11.976 --> 00:07:12.809
This is way back when.

00:07:12.809 --> 00:07:15.913
But there was an article about
using, changing something

00:07:15.913 --> 00:07:20.080
in how you save social security
from employees' paychecks.

00:07:20.977 --> 00:07:24.070
And I literally hadn't been
working there for three weeks.

00:07:24.070 --> 00:07:26.798
And so I sent it to
the CEO of Mellon Bank,

00:07:26.798 --> 00:07:28.300
hey, I thought you might
be interested in this.

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:29.942
Literally some guy he'd never heard of.

00:07:29.942 --> 00:07:31.597
And I got a note back, said thank you,

00:07:31.597 --> 00:07:33.182
and I'm thinking, okay,
look, my whole job,

00:07:33.182 --> 00:07:35.945
my whole motive in taking
a job, working for somebody

00:07:35.945 --> 00:07:37.849
was to help them become more profitable.

00:07:37.849 --> 00:07:39.155
That's the way I always looked at it.

00:07:39.155 --> 00:07:41.168
And then I started a rookies' club

00:07:41.168 --> 00:07:44.185
where I reached out to some
executives and all the people

00:07:44.185 --> 00:07:46.237
who had started at the
same time at Mellon Bank.

00:07:46.237 --> 00:07:47.442
We'd all go out and get a drink

00:07:47.442 --> 00:07:49.917
and we'd get this executive started.

00:07:49.917 --> 00:07:52.904
And I didn't, like, run it
through my boss or anything.

00:07:52.904 --> 00:07:56.167
And so my boss at the
time calls me in and says,

00:07:56.167 --> 00:07:57.781
you're doing this, and
I said yeah, thank you,

00:07:57.781 --> 00:07:58.833
and he was going to say, great.

00:07:58.833 --> 00:08:01.979
He just starts screaming
at me and reaming me.

00:08:01.979 --> 00:08:02.812
"How could you do this?

00:08:02.812 --> 00:08:04.956
You go everything through
me, you work for me."

00:08:04.956 --> 00:08:06.875
Yeah, so I knew I wasn't destined there.

00:08:06.875 --> 00:08:08.635
And then I left.

00:08:08.635 --> 00:08:09.699
(crowd laughs)

00:08:09.699 --> 00:08:13.957
And I went down to,
ended up down in Dallas

00:08:13.957 --> 00:08:15.778
and got a job working for a company

00:08:15.778 --> 00:08:17.217
called Your Business Software.

00:08:17.217 --> 00:08:18.967
And we sold software.

00:08:20.417 --> 00:08:21.869
And I didn't know anything about software.

00:08:21.869 --> 00:08:23.832
I took one computer class at Indiana

00:08:23.832 --> 00:08:26.405
and kind of cheated to pass.

00:08:26.405 --> 00:08:29.155
But not that I condone that, but.

00:08:30.319 --> 00:08:32.902
(crowd laughs)

00:08:33.799 --> 00:08:38.258
And so I had a customer, and
I'd stay up all night reading.

00:08:38.258 --> 00:08:39.745
I figured, you know, computers were new,

00:08:39.745 --> 00:08:42.264
and technology was new,
this technology was new.

00:08:42.264 --> 00:08:43.940
So nobody had a head start on me,

00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:46.318
'cause it was changing so
rapidly like it is today

00:08:46.318 --> 00:08:47.590
that if I just put in the time,

00:08:47.590 --> 00:08:49.302
I can learn as much as anybody else.

00:08:49.302 --> 00:08:50.551
And so I taught myself.

00:08:50.551 --> 00:08:53.905
I'd stay up reading software manuals,

00:08:53.905 --> 00:08:56.690
taught myself different little
simple programming languages

00:08:56.690 --> 00:08:59.274
and kept on getting bigger and
bigger and better and better.

00:08:59.274 --> 00:09:00.723
But it was at a retail store,

00:09:00.723 --> 00:09:02.969
and one of my responsibilities
was to come in,

00:09:02.969 --> 00:09:05.531
sweep the floor, wipe down the
windows, and open the store.

00:09:05.531 --> 00:09:07.527
And I had a customer who wanted me

00:09:07.527 --> 00:09:09.403
to come out there and close a deal.

00:09:09.403 --> 00:09:13.338
And it was a $15,000 deal,
$1,500 commission to me.

00:09:13.338 --> 00:09:14.842
Told my boss, he said, no,

00:09:14.842 --> 00:09:16.581
you need to be there to open the store.

00:09:16.581 --> 00:09:18.505
And I made the executive decision

00:09:18.505 --> 00:09:20.171
that I was going to go get the check.

00:09:20.171 --> 00:09:22.017
'Cause, you know, I was living six guys

00:09:22.017 --> 00:09:24.354
in a three-bedroom apartment,
sleeping on the floor,

00:09:24.354 --> 00:09:26.542
and this check meant I could, like not use

00:09:26.542 --> 00:09:28.568
the same Holiday Inn
towels with holes in them

00:09:28.568 --> 00:09:30.675
that I had stolen, you know.

00:09:30.675 --> 00:09:33.316
But anyway, I went and picked
up the check, thank you.

00:09:33.316 --> 00:09:35.656
When I came back and gave
the check to my boss,

00:09:35.656 --> 00:09:39.517
all would be forgiven, and,
you know, the sales cures all.

00:09:39.517 --> 00:09:40.755
Fired me.

00:09:40.755 --> 00:09:41.588
Fired me.

00:09:41.588 --> 00:09:44.599
And so those back-to-back
experiences confirmed

00:09:44.599 --> 00:09:47.655
what I already knew, that
I was a shitty-ass employee

00:09:47.655 --> 00:09:49.025
and that I'd better start my own.

00:09:49.025 --> 00:09:50.956
(crowd laughs)

00:09:50.956 --> 00:09:51.789
(whoosh)

00:09:51.789 --> 00:09:52.622
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:09:52.622 --> 00:09:54.190
- Now, this video was made because

00:09:54.190 --> 00:09:56.642
Jose Bergos asked Evan to on YouTube.

00:09:56.642 --> 00:09:58.262
If you have a famous entrepreneur

00:09:58.262 --> 00:10:00.292
that you really want
Evan to make a video of,

00:10:00.292 --> 00:10:02.075
leave it in the comment below.

00:10:02.075 --> 00:10:04.095
- I'd also love to know
which of Mark Cuban's

00:10:04.095 --> 00:10:06.638
top 10 rules had the
biggest impact on you.

00:10:06.638 --> 00:10:07.578
Leave it in the comments

00:10:07.578 --> 00:10:09.047
and we're going to join in the discussion.

00:10:09.047 --> 00:10:10.062
Thank you so much for watching.

00:10:10.062 --> 00:10:12.891
Continue to believe,
and we'll see you soon.

00:10:12.891 --> 00:10:14.025
(whoosh)

00:10:14.025 --> 00:10:15.441
(thump)

